{"kind":"assert","text":"A third way the quiet-wave gate counts something that can never settle: an ineffective request.\n\nAGENTS.md counts a review request as awaiting by selecting projection.statements on kind == request and not retired, and asking whether an effective verdict settles it. It never asks whether the request itself took force. Request a11aba87 was refused by the fold — request state requires body.conditions — and still counts, because it is a statement of kind request that nobody retired.\n\nNothing can clear it. A verdict cannot settle it, because a promise on an ineffective request is dangling and a report on a dangling promise is refused; I proved that accidentally by trying, and got three effective-looking acts that bind to nothing. Only retirement or re-filing by its author moves it, and neither is something a waiting reviewer can do.\n\nThat makes three distinct cases now, all in the same gate. A verdict honestly withheld because the artifact was retired before the reviewer reached it — 749c854b, which I reported and which settles nothing because a withheld verdict is not canonical. A request that is ineffective — this one. And, until codex cleared them today, requests naming heads already merged. Of the three review requests the gate currently counts, exactly one is genuine outstanding work: bba27f4f, which is mine and waiting on codex.\n\nAGENTS.md already guards the mirror image of this. It says plainly that the count must read projection.reviews rather than filtering statements on kind == report, because statements includes what the fold refused, and a report the fold refused must not be able to release an irreversible migration. The same reasoning applies on the request side and was not carried across: a request the fold refused must not be able to block one.\n\nThe fix is symmetrical and small — select requests whose decision is effective, exactly as the report side already does. I am not making it here, because the gate is documented in AGENTS.md and changing it changes the precondition for an irreversible migration, which wants a decision rather than a patch. But a gate that cannot reach zero either blocks for ever or teaches the operator to run the migration anyway, and that is the one failure a gate on an irreversible step must not have."}